{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a pragmatic, non-inductive rule of action: we prefer the theory that has withstood the most severe tests not because it is more likely true, but because it has proven itself the most robust instrument for survival and problem-solving so far.","counterconditions":["If the preference implies any increase in subjective probability or rational expectation of future success, it collapses into induction.","If Popper cannot distinguish between 'acting on' and 'believing in' without invoking probability, the defense fails."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Evolutionary Epistemology applied to theory choice: Just as organisms are selected for fitness without implying they will remain fit, theories are selected for corroboration without implying future truth. The 'preference' is a bet on the currently fittest survivor, acknowledging the risk of future refutation.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is indefensible on Popper's strict terms and necessarily smuggles in induction; the only consistent Popperian stance is radical skepticism where no theory is preferred for action over another unless one has been falsified and the other has not.","counterconditions":["If Popper admits any non-falsified theory can be acted upon equally, his system becomes practically useless for science.","If the 'smuggled induction' is unconscious, the system is psychologically impossible for humans to follow."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Logical Strictness: Any move from 'has survived' to 'should be used' entails a premise that 'the future will resemble the past' or 'nature is uniform,' which is the definition of induction. Without this hidden premise, the preference is arbitrary.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma dissolves if we view corroboration not as a measure of truth or probability, but as a measure of 'informational content' and 'testability'; we prefer the highly corroborated theory because it is the most informative and precise tool available, despite knowing it is likely false.","counterconditions":["If the preferred theory is known to be false, acting on it for critical applications (e.g., bridge building) seems irrational unless some probability is assigned.","If 'informational content' is valued because it leads to truth, this valuing process may implicitly rely on inductive hopes."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Content-Based Preference: High corroboration implies high empirical content (boldness). We prefer bold theories because they tell us more about the world, even if we expect them to be eventually refuted. The preference is for 'depth of insight' rather than 'likelihood of truth'.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's system contains a performative contradiction: the rejection of induction is itself a universal claim that cannot be corroborated without using the very inductive practices it forbids, rendering the 'defense' of non-inductive preference circular.","counterconditions":["If Popper treats his own anti-induction stance as a conjecture open to refutation, the circle might be broken.","If the critique applies to all philosophy, it loses specific force against Popper."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Self-Referential Incoherence: To claim 'induction is invalid' universally requires an inductive leap from past failures of induction to its future impossibility. Thus, any preference derived from this system inherits this foundational circularity.","optional_refs":["948d64104a5f02dbdb882cb6ffcb09517a41c51d482f7aeb84556ff18604d535"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45}],"simulation_proposals":[]}